# um-texts 1
=== allod gipat
Gipath
Gipath is an isolated no man's land. It is inhabited mainly by Orcs and Goblins and other unfriendly creatures. The only human settlement is very small and under constant threat of attack. 

=== allod gipat2
Gipath
Gipath - a neglected land not belonging to a single state. Much of the land is wilderness. Goblins, orcs and lizardmen can be found here, although there are also two small human settlements in the western part of the continent. Almost all of Gipath is littered with the ruins of buildings left behind by the long lost Joon race. It is said that this world was the site of a meteorite fall, for which a Khadagan expedition was sent in search.
=== allod ingos
Ingos
Ingos is a part of the Canian Empire. Its territory is vast and sparsely populated, so it is also inhabited by other races and tribes. Ingos is divided into districts governed by governors, who are assisted by guards sent from the capital. However, the inhabitants of Ingos enjoy relative freedom. They are famous among other peoples for their love of freedom and disrespect for any authority. 

=== allod jigran
Jygran
Jygran is a world of eternal autumn, a battlefield for two great empires fiercely hostile to each other - Khadagan and Cania. This land is ruled by necrosis and death. It is the result of the terrible spells of the mad necromancer Firiez. From a once flourishing land, evil spells have sucked out almost all the strength: Fields fail to yield, plants die, animals have no young. The population is basically only gathered in the ofortified cities, while the space beyond the reach of the walls is put at the mercy of various creatures and the undead, who can be found there more often than living beings. It is a closed world, lost in time and space.
=== allod suslanger
Suslanger
Suslanger belongs to the Khagadan Empire. Its territory is small, and the land suitable for cultivation is constantly decreasing. The river carving a deep canyon flows into a lake, which is the only source of drinking water. To survive in these inhospitable lands, the Suslanger people have built many dams and irrigation channels. Imperial authority is enforced in a brutal and ruthless manner. The guards mete out harsh punishments for even the most trivial offenses, while spies and informers report any deviation from the norm to the authorities. 

